            "alt": "Refer to caption",
            "desc": "5.1 The dissociation\n\n\nPre-error instability did not differ by PD severity (r=0.164r=0.164, p=0.413p=0.413, n=27n=27). Post-error recovery, modeled via log-normal AFT, showed a strong, highly significant association with UPDRS severity (coefficient =0.0252=0.0252, p=1.6×10−17p=1.6\\times 10^{-17} at the event level). Figure 1 shows both relationships directly.\n\n\nFigure 1: Pre-error instability (panel A, flat, not significant) versus post-error recovery time (panel B, clear positive trend with UPDRS-III severity), at the subject level, for the 27 PD subjects with UPDRS scores.\n\n\nCritically, the two measures are themselves uncorrelated (r=−0.065r=-0.065, p=0.748p=0.748), and in a joint OLS model predicting UPDRS from both simultaneously, post-error recovery remained highly significant (coefficient =34.93=34.93, p<0.001p<0.001) while pre-error instability did not (coefficient =20.06=20.06, p=0.120p=0.120). This confirms a genuine dissociation, not merely two findings of differing statistical power: the two measures capture independent information, and only one carries a detectable relationship to disease severity, even when both are given equal opportunity to compete for the same variance. The pre-error null was stable across alternative window sizes (2, 4, and 5 keystrokes; all p>0.3p>0.3), ruling out the specific 3-keystroke window choice as the explanation.",
